1: # SPEC: Phase 5 — Messaging Resilience
3: Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and Realtime 
outages, reloads, several tabs, account changes, and several devices; typing, unread/read state, and @mention 
notifications stay live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing 
existing messaging.
7: ## 1. Summary
9: The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state 
in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`, stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and 
opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with 
server-fenced attempt generations and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; 
application-level recovery and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, 
members-only typing indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including 
rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open 
conversation after reload.
11: ## 2. Problem statement
13: Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails 
must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it (the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and 
nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Optimistic messages are matched to stored ones by guessing from 
content and time. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK retries errored/timed-out channels and the 
conversation-list stream invalidates the list after its own rejoins, but the application exposes health only for the 
messages channel, does not recover terminal closures of the conversation-list channel, has no aggregate health, and 
has no polling fallback, so new messages and unread counts can silently stop updating. The typing indicator disappears 
after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public channel. Reading on one device leaves stale unread badges 
on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so room conversations (which never notify) cannot reach a 
specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
15: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
17: ### Goals
20: - G3: Messaging recovers from network and Realtime outages without user action and keeps updating through polling 
while Realtime is down.
21: - G4: Typing indicators are reliable, named, and visible only to conversation members.
22: - G5: Unread/read state and own sent messages stay in sync across the same user's tabs and devices (≤ 5 s while 
connectivity is healthy).
27: ### Success metrics
29: - With Realtime down and HTTP working, other members' new messages appear within 45 s of the outage start, and 
within 15 s (plus request time) of being sent once polling is active.
30: - With healthy connectivity and Realtime, cross-device/tab sync of unread counts, read state, and own sent 
messages within 5 s (D4); during a Realtime outage the polling deadlines above apply.
36: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
38: ### Primary users
41: ### Secondary users
45: ### Stakeholders
48: ## 5. Scope
50: ### In scope
54: - Application-level recovery, health, and catch-up for every messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation).
55: - Polling fallback after > 30 s of a required stream outage, while the tab is visible.
56: - Typing indicator: continuous display, names, DM/group/room, private members-only channel, own sessions excluded, 
expiry.
62: ### Out of scope
70: - Immediate revocation of typing signals for a tampered client of a removed member (accepted residual risk ≤ 
access-token lifetime, D3).
75: ## 6. User journeys
81: - J5 Sign-out: A clicks Sair with 2 unsent messages; a dialog warns that leaving deletes them; Cancelar leaves A 
exactly where A was; Sair mesmo signs A out.
83: - J7 Realtime outage: after 5 s the banner shows "Reconectando…"; after 30 s the open conversation and the list 
update every 15 s; when Realtime returns, polling stops and the banner disappears.
84: - J8 Typing: B types continuously in a group; A sees "Bruno está digitando…" the whole time; it disappears ≤ 5 s 
after B stops or leaves.
85: - J9 Two devices: A reads part of conversation X on the laptop; within 5 s the desktop's badge and read state for 
X match.
89: ## 7. Functional requirements
93: | FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested 
mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. If the durable write fails, nothing is dispatched 
(FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
96: | FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the 
oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). 
A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request 
timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt 
that started before or during the outage is superseded immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by 
whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the 
same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
98: | FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence 
request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). The server accepts a create only if its generation 
is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the moment of commit, and a fence with generation g 
atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to 
commit — including requests still in the network that have not reached the server; a fence or create with a generation 
older than the server's current one is rejected. When an attempt ends without a usable response (network error, 
timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, 
or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
106: | FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; 
"Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been 
unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 
|
107: | FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open 
conversation) has application-level health and recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins 
without user action using exponential backoff with a capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries); on recovery 
the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the typing stream 
rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
108: | FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages, the receipt summaries of its loaded messages, any open 
reader-details list, and the conversation list every 15 s; on becoming visible while still unhealthy it fetches 
immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all streams are healthy no new polling request starts. 
Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and never replaces newer or enriched state 
(attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older data. | Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
109: | FR-017 | The typing indicator stays visible while the other person types continuously and disappears at most 5 
s after they stop, blur the composer, send, leave the conversation, or close the tab. | Must | AC-017 |
110: | FR-018 | The indicator names typists: 1 → "Ana está digitando…", 2 → "Ana e Bruno estão digitando…", 3+ → "N 
pessoas estão digitando…", in direct, group, and room conversations. | Must | AC-018 |
111: | FR-019 | Typing signals travel only on a private Realtime topic whose join and send are authorized for current 
conversation members; anon clients, authenticated non-members, and public-topic attempts never receive them; the app 
detects the user's own removal from a conversation through a signal that does not depend on still having access to 
that conversation and leaves the topic within 5 s. Payload: user identity and typing flag only. Residual risk accepted 
(D3, D6): a tampered client of a removed member may receive and send typing signals until its access token expires. | 
Must | AC-019 |
112: | FR-020 | A user never sees their own typing indicator, including from their other tabs or devices. | Must | 
AC-020 |
113: | FR-021 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, within 5 s and without reload, focus change, or 
reconnect: when the user reads messages (fully or partly) on one tab/device, their other tabs/devices show the same 
unread counts and read state, and the sender's receipt summary/details agree with the stored receipts on all of the 
sender's sessions. During an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-021 |
114: | FR-022 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, a message the user sends from one device/tab appears on 
their other devices/tabs within 5 s without reload; during an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-022 |
115: | FR-023 | In the composer, typing "@" opens a list of the conversation's current members except the user, 
filtered as they type, operable by mouse and keyboard (arrows, Enter/Tab, Esc); choosing inserts a highlighted 
mention. In a room the list contains only members of the room conversation. Typed "@name" text that was not chosen 
from the list is plain text. | Must | AC-023, AC-024 |
119: | FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency (press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send 
outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage duration, polling activations and duration — in a 
bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at 
most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user 
rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, 
drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
128: ## 8. Business rules
136: | BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client 
session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 
5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 (e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a 
stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; 
reviews r2 #2, #11 |
140: | BR-009 | Typing: only current conversation members join and send on a conversation's typing topic; a user's own 
typing (any session) is never shown to them; an indicator expires ≤ 5 s after the last signal; the app leaves a 
conversation's topic within 5 s of the user's removal; residual exposure (receive and send) of a tampered 
removed-member client is bounded by access-token lifetime (D3, D6). | R1-q8 A, D, E; R1-q16 E; D3; D6 |
143: | BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab 
is visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 
A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
149: ## 9. Data and integrations
151: ### Data inputs
153: - Browser connectivity events, Realtime stream status, tab visibility, Supabase auth state changes.
156: ### Data outputs
158: - Database: attempt-generation state per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced or stale 
generation cannot commit; the original submitted composition used for replay comparison; mention records written 
atomically with the message; private typing-topic authorization (RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` → `users.id` membership); `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; 
`get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE; rate-limit buckets for all create requests and for fence/lookup calls, 
separate from the insertion quota.
163: ### Integrations
168: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
170: ### Constraints
179: - Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: 
claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events / 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s metrics 
abort.
181: ### Assumptions
184: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
192: | Hung holder when connectivity returns | Its attempt is superseded at once; D1 deadline holds (AC-041). |
194: | Owning tab closes or hangs mid-request | Closed or lease expired: another tab fences and finishes once; while 
the lease is live no other tab dispatches (AC-041). |
209: | Realtime drop < 5 s | No "Reconectando…" banner (AC-014). |
210: | Only the conversation-list stream fails | Banner after 5 s and polling after 30 s follow its health; recovered 
independently (AC-015, AC-016). |
211: | Stale poll returns after a newer Realtime update | Newer/enriched state kept; no duplicates (AC-048). |
212: | Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s (AC-016). |
213: | Typist closes the tab or leaves the conversation mid-typing | Indicator disappears ≤ 5 s (AC-017). |
214: | Anon, outsider, public-topic, or removed-member typing access | Refused / no events; removed member's app 
leaves at once; tampered client bounded by D3 (AC-019). |
218: | Partial reads by others during a Realtime-only outage | Receipt summaries and open reader details update via 
polling (AC-016). |
223: ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
227: - Typing topics are private and members-only for join and send (Realtime RLS with `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` → `users.id`); the app leaves within 5 s of a removal; residual receive/send exposure of tampered 
removed-member clients accepted (D3, D6).
235: ## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
236: - UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out dialog text, typing phrases, and mention 
notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", "enviando", "expirada"), actions 
("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 is the owner's D5 wording), and 
equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
242: ## 14. Acceptance criteria
244: - AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows 
it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
246: - AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network 
error after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
248: - AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in 
conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously and m1's real 
create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until m1 is resolved; the network logs of both 
tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, exactly once each; the database holds one row 
per key.
257: - AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages 
stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" 
appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user action.
258: - AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped independently (messages stream only; conversation-list 
stream only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), when Realtime becomes available again, then that 
stream recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the outage appear once; unread counts are correct; 
typing works again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped retry delays.
259: - AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, 
when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started 
appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update; when C reads part of A's earlier messages 
during the outage (no new message sent), A's receipt summaries and open reader-details list update within 15 s plus 
request time once polling is active. Given only the conversation-list stream is unhealthy for more than 30 s, polling 
starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when it becomes visible, a fetch happens 
immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message delivered by both appears once, and no new 
polling request starts during the next 60 s.
260: - AC-017 (FR-017; must-not R1-q16 E): Given B types continuously for 15 s, then A sees the typing indicator at 
every 1 s sample; when B stops, it disappears within 5 s; when B closes the tab mid-typing, within 5 s; when B 
switches to another conversation mid-typing, within 5 s.
261: - AC-018 (FR-018): Given a group or room with A, B, C, D, when B types, A sees "<B> está digitando…"; B and C → 
"<B> e <C> estão digitando…"; B, C, D → "3 pessoas estão digitando…"; a direct conversation shows the single-typist 
phrase.
262: - AC-019 (FR-019, BR-009): With real local Realtime: an anon client, an authenticated non-member, and a client on 
the public topic name receive no typing events of conversation X and cannot send typing events members receive; member 
A receives B's typing; when member E is removed from X while subscribed (and while X is not open in E's drawer), E's 
app leaves the topic within 5 s and shows no further typing for X (integration + E2E).
263: - AC-020 (FR-020, BR-009): Given A has two tabs and a second device on X, when A types in tab 1, then neither tab 
2 nor the second device shows A as typing.
264: - AC-021 (FR-021): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices with 6 unread in X, when A reads only the 
first 3 on device 1, then within 5 s device 2 shows 3 unread for X; after reading the rest, 0. Given A is the sender 
of messages read by B, then A's receipt summary/details on both of A's sessions match the stored receipts within 5 s.
265: - AC-022 (FR-022): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices, when A sends on device 1, then it appears 
once on device 2 within 5 s without reload.
271: - AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route 
writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, and server-derived version; a scan finds no message 
text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, 
or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; a request over 50 events or 16 KB is rejected; requests 
beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an unauthenticated request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
272: - AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and separately hangs, and separately is flooded with sustained 
traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most one metrics 
request is in flight from A's tab, a hanging metrics request is aborted after 5 s, the client buffer never exceeds its 
bound (oldest events dropped), and no error is shown.
279: - AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and in Edge; the key flows (outbox, 
banner, typing, mentions) were verified in Firefox and recorded; no unit test was added; every touched unit test was 
replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
280: - AC-037 (FR-014, §13): The banner is announced via a polite live region, item states have text labels, and the 
sign-out dialog and mention list are fully keyboard-operable.
281: - AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is stored but its response is dropped, then the item 
becomes unknown, the fence resolves it as delivered without a second create, and B sees it once.
283: - AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is held, when A closes the tab before the server 
confirms, then on reopening the app the item is fenced and B sees the message exactly once.
284: - AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create request is held, then tab 2 sends no create request 
for that conversation while tab 1's lease is live; when tab 1 is closed (and separately when its lease expires while 
it hangs), tab 2 fences and the message is stored once. Given tab 1's attempt hangs across an outage, when 
connectivity returns with that single item pending and quota available, then it is delivered within 10 s (D1) and 
stored once.
285: - AC-042 (FR-006, FR-009): Given an unknown item whose request was actually stored, when A tries to cancel it or 
its 24 h passes, then no cancel is offered before fencing, the fence resolves it as delivered, and it never shows 
"expirada".
288: - AC-045 (FR-006, BR-004): With real local Postgres and the real routes: (a) attempt 1's INSERT is blocked (row 
lock held by the test) while a fence for that key reports not stored; on release attempt 1 does not commit; (b) 
attempt 1's request is held before reaching the server while a fence runs; when released it is rejected as stale; (c) 
a stale fence (older generation) arriving after a newer attempt was dispatched is rejected and the newer attempt 
commits; in every case at most one message is stored (integration + E2E).
291: - AC-048 (FR-016): Given a poll response is held while Realtime delivers a newer state (a reaction, a receipt, an 
attachment message, a mention) and older history pages are loaded, when the stale poll is released, then the feed 
keeps the newer state, the loaded pages, and one bubble per message.
295: ## 15. Test strategy
298:   - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): sender and attempt-generation fencing 
incl. stale requests and stale fences (AC-044, AC-045), insertion quota, request budget, and fence budget (AC-009), 
fence/lookup authorization incl. anon and other users' keys (AC-038, AC-042, AC-044), outcome classes (AC-046), 
mention atomicity/validation/replay/RLS (AC-024, AC-046), private typing-topic authorization incl. 
anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), `get_unread_counts` anon revoke 
(AC-031), metrics route auth, enums, sizes, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
299:   - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two tabs of one account; a second device as a separate 
browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context 
offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; holding a request or response by delayed 
pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling 
IndexedDB or failing its writes in the page (AC-039). Old-build compatibility: the hosted and Phase 4 builds are run 
locally against the migrated database (AC-034). Expiry may use a test-controlled clock without changing the product 
rule.
305: - Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream 
Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and stale polls, typing tab close and conversation 
switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification 
race, delayed notification authorization, malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost 
access.
